        {"unit": "GUNIT89", "title": "Philosophy of Ubuntu", "canDo": "I can discuss Ubuntu", "dialoguePlace": "Village Circle", "readingText": "Ubuntu says: I am because we are. A person is people. My success is linked to yours. If my neighbour suffers, I suffer. Ubuntu teaches community, not selfishness. In East Africa we say Ujamaa - familyhood. Modern life pushes individualism. Ubuntu reminds us to return to togetherness.", "readingWords": 54, "vocab": ["Ubuntu", "Linked", "Selfishness", "Togetherness", "Individualism"], "grammar": "I am because we are", "tryAtHome": "Explain Ubuntu in English"},
        {"unit": "GUNIT90", "title": "Critical Essay: Development", "canDo": "I can write critical essay", "dialoguePlace": "University", "readingText": "Development is not only buildings. It is human freedom. A country can have tall buildings and still have hungry children. True development means children learn, mothers are healthy, farmers earn fairly. We must measure development by wellbeing, not only by GDP. East Africa must measure by people.", "readingWords": 56, "vocab": ["Development", "Freedom", "Wellbeing", "Measure", "Fairly"], "grammar": "Development is not only...", "tryAtHome": "Write paragraph on development"},
        {"unit": "GUNIT91", "title": "Storytelling as Power", "canDo": "I can analyse storytelling", "dialoguePlace": "Library", "readingText": "Stories shape nations. Grandmothers stories taught values. Today, films and social media tell stories. Who tells the story controls the narrative. East Africans must tell their own stories. In English and Swahili. Our stories are not poverty. Our stories are resilience, drums, Kilimanjaro, lakes, and hope.", "readingWords": 58, "vocab": ["Narrative", "Controls", "Resilience", "Values", "Shape"], "grammar": "Who tells... controls...", "tryAtHome": "Tell your story in 3 min"},
        {"unit": "GUNIT92", "title": "Research Skills", "canDo": "I can do research", "dialoguePlace": "Library", "readingText": "Research starts with a question. Find sources. Compare sources. Take notes. Cite sources. Do not copy. Think. A good researcher is honest. If you use someone else's words, give credit. Research is not copying, it is creating understanding.", "readingWords": 52, "vocab": ["Research", "Sources", "Cite", "Credit", "Understanding"], "grammar": "Find / Compare / Cite", "tryAtHome": "Research 1 topic"},
        {"unit": "GUNIT93", "title": "Leadership Speech", "canDo": "I can write speech", "dialoguePlace": "Hall", "readingText": "Fellow East Africans, we stand at a crossroads. Our hills are green. Our youth are bright. Our future is ours. We must choose honesty over corruption, work over excuses, unity over division. Let us build an East Africa where every child learns, every farmer earns, every voice counts. The time is now.", "readingWords": 56, "vocab": ["Fellow", "Crossroads", "Unity", "Division", "Counts"], "grammar": "We must choose...", "tryAtHome": "Write 1 min speech"},
        {"unit": "GUNIT94", "title": "Business Plan", "canDo": "I can write business plan", "dialoguePlace": "Incubator", "readingText": "A business plan answers: What problem? Who is customer? What is solution? How will you earn? How much do you need? Mary plans an English school for East African adults and children. Customers: adults and students in 8 countries. Price: 1000 shillings per month. She needs 500 customers to sustain. John will manage operations. Plan makes dreams real.", "readingWords": 64, "vocab": ["Business plan", "Customer", "Sustain", "Problem", "Solution"], "grammar": "What problem? Who is customer?", "tryAtHome": "Draft business plan"},
        {"unit": "GUNIT95", "title": "Diplomacy", "canDo": "I can talk about diplomacy", "dialoguePlace": "Embassy", "readingText": "Diplomacy is talking, not fighting. Countries negotiate trade, peace, and climate. A diplomat listens, speaks carefully, and builds bridges. East Africa needs diplomats who speak English, French, and Swahili. Language is diplomacy. Words can prevent war.", "readingWords": 52, "vocab": ["Diplomacy", "Negotiate", "Bridges", "Prevent", "Carefully"], "grammar": "Diplomacy is talking...", "tryAtHome": "Role play diplomacy"},
        {"unit": "GUNIT96", "title": "Teaching Others", "canDo": "I can teach", "dialoguePlace": "Classroom", "readingText": "The best way to learn is to teach. When Mary teaches John English, she learns twice. She prepares. She explains. She answers questions. She becomes confident. Teaching is leadership. If you know 10 words, teach 10 words. Knowledge grows when shared. John now teaches his staff in Nairobi English too.", "readingWords": 60, "vocab": ["Teach", "Confident", "Explains", "Shared", "Grows"], "grammar": "The best way to learn is to teach", "tryAtHome": "Teach 5 words to someone"}
